Kentucky congressman Thomas Massie moved on Monday to force a vote in the House of Representatives to impeach Defence Secretary Pete Hegseth, putting pressure on his fellow Republicans to decide whether to remove one of the Trump administration’s most prominent figures following the party's crushing defeat in midterm elections.
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/3193d7d8-c644-11f1-b677-3774aeac812a/w:1024/p:16x9/AP26279700094442.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/3193d7d8-c644-11f1-b677-3774aeac812a/w:1024/p:16x9/AP26279700094442.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">531873f6-dc73-11f1-b71c-33c7ce18607e</guid><pubDate>Mon, 09 Nov 2026 19:32:26 GMT</pubDate><source url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/3193d7d8-c644-11f1-b677-3774aeac812a/w:1024/p:16x9/AP26279700094442.jpg">© Tom Brenner, AP</source><dc:creator>FRANCE 24</dc:creator></item><item><category>Tête à tête</category><title>'We think' the Iranians and Americans want to negotiate, Egyptian FM says</title><link>https://www.france24.com/en/tv-shows/t%C3%AAte-%C3%A0-t%C3%AAte/20261109-we-think-the-iranians-and-americans-want-to-negotiate-egyptian-fm-says</link><description>
In an interview with FRANCE 24, Egyptian Foreign Minister Badr Abdelatty said he believed Iran and the United States wanted to negotiate, but called for a "concrete deal" to prevent another escalation. He also said Cairo was still "counting" on US President Donald Trump to "move forward" with the stalled Gaza peace plan and was "confident" US military aid to Egypt would continue. 
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/b7f4021e-dc3b-11f1-a48b-bf1914cabd52/w:1024/p:16x9/MAE-EGYPTIEN-PLAN-INVITE-2026-11-09-F24.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/b7f4021e-dc3b-11f1-a48b-bf1914cabd52/w:1024/p:16x9/MAE-EGYPTIEN-PLAN-INVITE-2026-11-09-F24.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">71874660-dc27-11f1-b46c-fbffa696f57a</guid><pubDate>Mon, 09 Nov 2026 19:31:42 GMT</pubDate><dc:creator>Marc PERELMAN</dc:creator></item><item><category></category><title>France opens probe into Iranian cyber unit over fridge attacks</title><link>https://www.france24.com/en/france-opens-probe-into-iranian-cyber-unit-over-fridge-attacks</link><description>
France on Monday said Iran's Revolutionary Guards were behind "unprecedented" cyberattacks on supermarket fridges in the country last month, vowing sanctions on a European level against Tehran. Swathes of French supermarkets were hit in the October 11 and 12 cyberattacks, which raised the temperatures in their refrigerators, in some cases forcing them to throw away entire inventories of perishable food.
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/2c262730-dc72-11f1-912e-a575454bc579/w:1024/p:16x9/EN-20261109-200113-200918-CS.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/2c262730-dc72-11f1-912e-a575454bc579/w:1024/p:16x9/EN-20261109-200113-200918-CS.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">31975a18-dc72-11f1-82fd-2555ae672825</guid><pubDate>Mon, 09 Nov 2026 19:24:54 GMT</pubDate><dc:creator>FRANCE24</dc:creator></item><item><category>The Debate</category><title>Broken system? French protesters rage against austerity</title><link>https://www.france24.com/en/tv-shows/the-debate/20261109-broken-system-french-protesters-rage-against-austerity</link><description>
How can the nation of haute couture and high speed rail have an affordability issue? On paper, France boasts the planet’s seventh largest economy. Its top diplomat started the week hosting a Group of Seven Industrialized Nations foreign ministers meeting inside the regal setting of its landmark Left Bank ministry.
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/3b2413b6-dc70-11f1-9a7c-d18c5759e759/w:1024/p:16x9/2026-11-07T151455Z-100900085-RC23JOAPD1O1-RTRMADP-3-FRANCE-PROTEST.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/3b2413b6-dc70-11f1-9a7c-d18c5759e759/w:1024/p:16x9/2026-11-07T151455Z-100900085-RC23JOAPD1O1-RTRMADP-3-FRANCE-PROTEST.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">364a27d2-dc6f-11f1-a558-9d9e76ee6a52</guid><pubDate>Mon, 09 Nov 2026 19:19:18 GMT</pubDate><dc:creator>François PICARD</dc:creator></item><item><category></category><title>'Life in Gaza simply miserable': Amid flooding, lack of aid, conditions 'worsen' for Palestinians</title><link>https://www.france24.com/en/life-in-gaza-simply-miserable-amid-flooding-lack-of-aid-conditions-worsen-for-palestinians</link><description>
Erin Ogunkeye is pleased to welcome Shaina Low, Communication Adviser for the Norwegian Refugee Council (NRC). Thirteen months into what she describes as a "so-called ceasefire," the humanitarian crisis in Gaza continues to deepen, exposing the profound gap between diplomatic commitments and the lived realities of Palestinian civilians. In an interview with France 24, Shayna Low, communications adviser in Palestine for the Norwegian Refugee Council (NRC), describes a population confronting winter flooding, repeated displacement, deteriorating shelter conditions and persistent restrictions on humanitarian assistance. "Life in Gaza is simply miserable." Low urges Israel to "open all the crossings, lift the restrictions, let aid in."
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/e3d3edca-dc60-11f1-8648-695cb59fade5/w:1024/p:16x9/EN-20261109-160607-161526-CS.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/e3d3edca-dc60-11f1-8648-695cb59fade5/w:1024/p:16x9/EN-20261109-160607-161526-CS.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">e94ce432-dc60-11f1-96d5-cb6fc6fe985c</guid><pubDate>Mon, 09 Nov 2026 17:26:51 GMT</pubDate><dc:creator>FRANCE24</dc:creator></item></channel></rss>
